### Fixing the Issue

Based on your setup (explicit `MongoClient` and `MongoTemplate`), here’s how to ensure `MongoTemplate` uses your Atlas cluster instead of `localhost`:

#### Solution 1: Correct `MongoClient` Configuration

Update `FlowConfig` to ensure the `MongoClient` is properly configured and tied to `MongoTemplate`:

```java
package com.example.config;

import com.mongodb.ConnectionString;
import com.mongodb.MongoClientSettings;
import com.mongodb.client.MongoClient;
import com.mongodb.client.MongoClients;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Value;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.config.EnableJpaRepositories;
import org.springframework.data.mongodb.core.MongoTemplate;
import org.springframework.ai.embedding.EmbeddingModel;
import org.springframework.ai.vectorstore.VectorStore;
import org.springframework.ai.vectorstore.mongodb.MongoDBAtlasVectorStore;

@Configuration
@EnableJpaRepositories(basePackages = "com.example.repository")
public class FlowConfig {

    @Value("${spring.data.mongodb.uri}")
    private String mongoUri;

    @Value("${spring.data.mongodb.database}")
    private String mongoDatabase;

    @Bean
    public MongoClient mongoClient() {
        ConnectionString connectionString = new ConnectionString(mongoUri);
        MongoClientSettings settings = MongoClientSettings.builder()
                .applyConnectionString(connectionString)
                .build();
        MongoClient client = MongoClients.create(settings);
        System.out.println("MongoClient Cluster: " + client.getClusterDescription());
        return client;
    }

    @Bean
    public MongoTemplate mongoTemplate(MongoClient mongoClient) {
        MongoTemplate template = new MongoTemplate(mongoClient, mongoDatabase);
        System.out.println("MongoTemplate Connection: " + template.getDb().getMongoClient().getAllAddress());
        return template;
    }

    @Bean(name = "defaultVectorStore")
    public VectorStore defaultVectorStore(MongoTemplate mongoTemplate, EmbeddingModel embeddingModel) {
        System.out.println("VectorStore MongoTemplate Connection: " + mongoTemplate.getDb().getMongoClient().getAllAddress());
        return MongoDBAtlasVectorStore.builder()
                .withMongoTemplate(mongoTemplate)
                .withEmbeddingModel(embeddingModel)
                .withCollectionName("default_vector_collection")
                .build();
    }

    // ... other beans ...
}
```

- **Changes**:
  - Used `ConnectionString` and `MongoClientSettings` for explicit configuration.
  - Added debug prints to verify the connection at each step.
- **Properties**:
  ```properties
  spring.data.mongodb.uri=mongodb+srv://user:pass@xxxxx.mongodb.net/my_db?retryWrites=true&w=majority
  spring.data.mongodb.database=my_db
  ```

#### Solution 2: Handle Proxy/DNS Issues

Since you’re using a proxy for OpenAI, it might interfere with MongoDB’s SRV resolution:
- **Set JVM Proxy for MongoDB**:
  - Add system properties in your application startup:
    ```java
    @SpringBootApplication
    public class Application {
        public static void main(String[] args) {
            System.setProperty("http.proxyHost", "proxy.example.com");
            System.setProperty("http.proxyPort", "8080");
            System.setProperty("https.proxyHost", "proxy.example.com");
            System.setProperty("https.proxyPort", "8080");
            SpringApplication.run(Application.class, args);
        }
    }
    ```
  - Or via command line:
    ```bash
    java -Dhttp.proxyHost=proxy.example.com -Dhttp.proxyPort=8080 -Dhttps.proxyHost=proxy.example.com -Dhttps.proxyPort=8080 -jar your-app.jar
    ```
  - **Note**: MongoDB SRV resolution uses DNS over TCP/UDP, not HTTP, so an HTTP proxy might not help unless it’s a transparent network proxy.

- **Bypass Proxy for MongoDB**:
  - If your proxy doesn’t support DNS resolution for MongoDB Atlas, use a direct connection by adding your Atlas hosts to `noProxy`:
    ```java
    System.setProperty("http.nonProxyHosts", "*.mongodb.net");
    ```

- **Explicit Host List** (Alternative to SRV)**:
  - Replace the SRV URI with a standard URI listing all shard hosts (find these in your Atlas dashboard under "Connect" > "MongoDB Drivers"):
    ```properties
    spring.data.mongodb.uri=mongodb://user:pass@cluster0-shard-00-00.abc123.mongodb.net:27017,cluster0-shard-00-01.abc123.mongodb.net:27017/my_db?retryWrites=true&w=majority
    ```

#### Solution 3: Disable Auto-Configuration

Ensure no default `MongoTemplate` overrides your explicit one:
```java
@SpringBootApplication(exclude = {MongoAutoConfiguration.class, MongoDataAutoConfiguration.class})
public class Application {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(Application.class, args);
    }
}
```

- Then rely solely on your `mongoClient` and `mongoTemplate` beans.

